<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Chicago</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Chicago is a private research university in Chicago, founded in 1890 with funding from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-d-rockefeller">John D. Rockefeller</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-institute">Oriental Institute</a>, established by James Henry Breasted in 1919, has led excavations across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. The university sponsored Edgar Banks's dig at Adab in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 1903–1905 and, from 2006, excavations at Zincirli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, the site of ancient Sam'al.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago.jpg" alt="University of Chicago" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campus_Spring.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first University of Chicago was founded by a small group of Baptist educators and incorporated in 1857 after a land endowment from Senator Stephen A. Douglas. It closed in 1886 after decades of financial struggle, worsened by the Great Chicago Fire and the Panic of 1873, when its creditors foreclosed on its property. In 1890 its trustees renamed it the Old University of Chicago so that a new university could take the name of the city.</p><p>The American Baptist Education Society incorporated the new University of Chicago in 1890 as a coeducational institution. It used $400,000 donated to the society to supplement a $600,000 donation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-d-rockefeller">John D. Rockefeller</a>, co-founder of Standard Oil, and land given by Marshall Field. Wealthy Chicagoans financed the Hyde Park campus: Silas B. Cobb gave its first building, Cobb Lecture Hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the death of the first president in 1906, Harry Pratt Judson became president in 1907. His financial austerity renewed Rockefeller's confidence and brought large gifts between 1906 and 1910, including $10 million in 1910 that balanced the budget.</p><p>In 1929 Robert Maynard Hutchins, the 30-year-old dean of Yale Law School, became president. In 1930 he organised the graduate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> into four divisions and united the undergraduate colleges into one college, and in 1931 a two-year general education curriculum called the New Plan formed the basis of the core curriculum. The Metallurgical Laboratory produced the first human-made, self-sustaining nuclear reaction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chicago-pile-1">Chicago Pile-1</a>, beneath the viewing stands of Stagg Field. In 1978 Hanna Holborn Gray became the first woman in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> appointed to a full-term presidency of a major research university.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The university's scholars helped develop economics, law, literary criticism, mathematics, physics, religion, sociology and political science, and established the Chicago schools of thought in several fields. Its students, faculty and staff have included 102 Nobel laureates. It operates Fermilab and Argonne National Laboratory, and the University of Chicago Press is the largest university press in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The undergraduate college introduced a widely adopted model of liberal arts education that featured the Socratic method, the Great Books program and the core curriculum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Chicago” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…for establishing the principles that underlie American law, and replicas of the stele stand at the Headquarters of the United Nations in New York City, the Peace Palace in The Hague, the Pergamon Museum in Berlin and the <b>University of Chicago</b>'s Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Anthony C. Yu's four-volume translation of 1977 to 1983 was the first to render the poems and songs, which he argues are essential to the author's meaning, and the <b>University of Chicago</b> Press issued a revised edition in 2012.</blockquote><a href="/work/journey-to-the-west">Journey to the West · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>A prose translation based on the Critical Edition, begun by J. A. B. van Buitenen and continued by several scholars after his death, is still in progress at the <b>University of Chicago</b> Press, and the Clay Sanskrit Library has published 15 volumes of a projected 32.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Samuel Noah Kramer joined twenty-two fragments into the first complete edition, published by the <b>University of Chicago</b> in 1940.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the stands of Stagg Field at the <b>University of Chicago</b>, Enrico Fermi's team started a self-sustaining chain reaction in Chicago Pile-1.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Excavations by Edgar Banks for the <b>University of Chicago</b> in 1903 to 1905 found temple remains, statues and tablets, and many more Adab texts later entered collections through looting.</blockquote><a href="/state/adab">Adab</a></li></ul></section>
